 "intro": "Near Chaeronea in Boeotia, Greece, 86 BC, in the First Mithridatic War. Archelaus, general of King Mithridates of Pontus, has a huge army with scythed chariots and cavalry. You are Lucius Cornelius Sulla, with perhaps 30,000 men.",
 "debrief": "In Plutarch’s account (he came from Chaeronea) and Appian’s, Sulla dug palisades on his flanks against the cavalry, and two local men led a force up a hidden path to drive a Pontic post off Mount Thurium. Archelaus’s scythed chariots, launched from too short a distance, were stopped and killed, while the Romans clapped and called for more as at the races. The reserve held the left flank, and Sulla led the cavalry on the right. The Pontic army was routed. Sulla’s claim to have lost only fourteen men is not believed. The other choices are what-ifs.",
